const phaseCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · A map of states', note: 'Phase diagrams plot what phases exist at each temperature and composition: axes T and composition, regions labelled (liquid, solid solutions, two-phase mixes). Read the coordinates, get the microstructure — a thermodynamic map of the alloy.' },
    { math: '2 · The lever rule: how much of each', note: 'In a two-phase region, the amounts of each phase split by the tie-line\'s arms — the lever rule: composition sets the ratio, geometry of the diagram does the arithmetic. Steel\'s ferrite–cementite split falls out of one line drawing.' },
    { math: '3 · The eutectic: the magic composition', note: 'Where the liquid freezes to two solids at once at the lowest temperature (solder\'s tin–lead, cast iron\'s graphite). Eutectics melt low and cast beautifully — the compositions civilisations stumbled into for casting (bronze\'s tin content sits near useful eutectics).' },
    { math: '4 · Iron–carbon: the most important diagram in history', note: 'Steel is iron + 0.02–2% carbon; the diagram explains all of it: austenite (high-T phase dissolves carbon), ferrite (almost none), cementite (iron carbide — hard, brittle). Heat treatments (the next lesson) are itineraries ON this map.' },
  ],
};

const phaseQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'A 50:50 alloy sits in a two-phase field where the tie-line ends at 20% B (α) and 70% B (β). The fraction of β is…', choices: ['50%', '60%', '40%', '70%'], answer: 1, why: 'Lever rule: f(β) = (C₀ − Cα)/(Cβ − Cα) = (50 − 20)/(70 − 20) = 60%. The phase farther from the overall composition gets the larger fraction — the seesaw arithmetic.' },
    { q: 'A eutectic composition is prized for casting because it…', choices: ['is the strongest', 'freezes at a single low temperature, filling moulds cleanly', 'never corrodes', 'is the cheapest'], answer: 1, why: 'At the eutectic the liquid transforms to two solids at one fixed temperature — no wide mushy freezing range, sharp mould filling. 63/37 Sn–Pb solder and cast iron both sit near eutectics for exactly this reason.' },
    { q: 'In the iron–carbon system, cementite (Fe₃C) is…', choices: ['soft and ductile', 'a hard, brittle iron-carbide phase that provides much of steel\'s strength', 'a liquid phase', 'pure iron'], answer: 1, why: 'Cementite is Fe₃C — 6.7% carbon, extremely hard and brittle. Steel\'s properties come from ferrite (soft, tough) plus carefully distributed cementite, and heat treatment controls that distribution.' },
    { q: 'A LiFePO₄ cathode charges along a very flat voltage plateau (~3.45 V). The phase-diagram reason is…', choices: ['the battery regulates voltage electronically', 'two solid phases coexist during charging, so lithium\'s chemical potential stays constant', 'the voltage is set by the charger', 'flat plateaus only happen in liquids'], answer: 1, why: 'During a two-phase reaction the electrode is a mixture of Li-poor and Li-rich crystals, and coexistence pins the chemical potential — exactly the lever-rule physics, read out as voltage.' },
  ],
};

