      "left": "single-phase field, binary alloy (C = 2)",
      "right": "F = 2 — temperature and composition both vary freely"
    },
    {
      "left": "two-phase field",
      "right": "F = 1 — pick the temperature and both phase compositions are pinned"
    },
    {
      "left": "three-phase eutectic point (L + α + β)",
      "right": "F = 0 — a single temperature and composition, no freedom left"
    },
    {
      "left": "tie-line ends vs overall composition",
      "right": "the ends are fixed by temperature alone; only the proportions follow the overall composition"
    }
  ],
  "why": "That is the geometry behind the whole diagram: pinned endpoints, movable proportions — and the lever rule is just mass balance on the pinned line."
};

const pdSolubilityCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two ideal behaviours of a binary system — drag between them:",
  "before": {
    "label": "complete solid solubility",
    "text": "Cu–Ni: two atoms so alike that one crystal takes any mixture. The diagram is a lens between two melting lines — a single phase field, wall to wall."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "limited solubility",
    "text": "Most pairs: solid solutions survive only up to a solvus line, beyond which a second phase must appear. Cross that line and precipitates form — the basis of age-hardened aluminium."
  },
  "why": "Solubility limits are where strengthening begins: the solvus is the doorway from solid solution to obstacle field."
};

const pdWilmOrder = {
  "prompt": "Wilm's 1906 observation to modern aircraft skins — drag the story into order:",
  "items": [
    "1906 — a quenched Al–4% Cu alloy keeps strengthening for days at room temperature",
    "Nothing in the equilibrium maps of the era predicts it",
    "1938 — X-ray work reveals tiny copper-rich clusters: Guinier–Preston zones",
    "The quench had trapped a supersaturated solid solution — the solvus crossed in slow motion",
    "The clusters obstruct dislocations far more effectively than the coarse equilibrium phase could"
  ],
  "why": "Age hardening is metallurgy's most valuable piece of non-equilibrium behaviour — the solvus is the only trace it leaves on the equilibrium map."
