---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/phase-diagrams">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Materials science lesson: the point where atoms, bonds and crystals
    become alloys you can actually process. The foundational lesson Why materials behave gave
    you bonds and the origin of stiffness; the intermediate lessons Atoms to crystals
    (lattices, planes, diffraction) and Metals, ceramics, polymers and composites
    (dislocations, and how metals are strengthened) built the machinery this lesson assumes.
    Here the pieces assemble into a map — the thermodynamic map on which every heat treatment
    and every battery electrode is an itinerary.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define a phase and use the Gibbs phase rule to say what freedom a system has at each point of a diagram;</li>
    <li>read tie-lines and compute phase fractions with the lever rule, including the eutectoid numbers for steel;</li>
    <li>explain why eutectic compositions cast cleanly, and why civilisations found them before science explained them;</li>
    <li>navigate the iron–carbon diagram's three phases and connect them to steel grades and pearlite;</li>
    <li>read a battery's voltage curve as a phase diagram turned sideways, with plateaus as two-phase fields.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You have met atoms, bonds, crystals and defects; the phase diagram is where those pieces
    become <em>materials</em>. It answers the question every processing route silently relies
    on: at this temperature and this composition, what exists — one phase or several, and in
    what proportions? A phase diagram is a thermodynamic map: axes of temperature and
    composition, regions labelled with the equilibrium phases, and lines where phases
    transform. Metallurgy became a science when these maps were drawn, because blacksmith
    wisdom ("heat it till it's cherry-red, then quench") became itinerary planning on a chart.
    This unit's two lectures — phases, and the chemistry of batteries — are one subject: a
    battery electrode is a phase diagram read sideways, with voltage playing the role of
    temperature.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the map, the lever, the eutectic, iron–carbon" widget="equationStep" config={phaseCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels build the skill in order. Panel one is
    the map itself — axes of temperature and composition, regions labelled, lines where
    phases transform; panel two adds the tie-line arithmetic; panel three marks the
    compositions that freeze at a single temperature; panel four is the map history was made
    on. Step through them and watch the same geometry do different jobs: read coordinates in
    panel one, compute proportions in panel two, choose a casting composition in panel
    three, plan a heat treatment in panel four.
  </p>

  <h2>What a phase is, and the phase rule</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>phase</strong> is a region of matter that is uniform in structure and
    composition: solid, liquid and gas are phases, but so are two different crystals in one
    solid (ferrite and cementite in steel) — same element mixture, different arrangements.
    The <strong>Gibbs phase rule</strong> counts freedom: at constant pressure, F = C − P + 1,
    where C is the number of components and P the number of coexisting phases. For a binary
    alloy (C = 2) in a single-phase field, F = 2 — temperature and composition can both vary
    freely. In a two-phase field, F = 1: pick the temperature and the <em>compositions of the
    two phases are fixed</em>. That is the thermodynamic fact behind the diagram's geometry:
    the ends of the horizontal tie-line (the phase compositions) are pinned by temperature
    alone, and only their <em>proportions</em> depend on the overall composition. It is also,
    anticipating the battery section, why a three-phase invariant point (eutectic: L + α + β,
    F = 0) occurs at one exact temperature and composition — no freedom left.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each region of the map with its freedom" widget="match" config={pdFreedomMatch} />
  <p>
    The two ideal behaviours a binary system can show: <strong>complete solid solubility</strong>
    (Cu–Ni: the two atoms are so similar that one crystal accommodates any mixture — the
    diagram is a lens between two melting lines) and <strong>limited solubility</strong> (most
    pairs: solid solutions exist only up to a solubility limit, beyond which a second phase
    must appear). Solubility limits are where strengthening comes from: precipitates that form
    when you cross a solvus line are the basis of age-hardening aluminium alloys (the defects
    lesson's dislocations meet their obstacles here).
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag between the lens and the solvus" widget="compare" config={pdSolubilityCompare} />
  <p>
    The classic case study is the one that made the aerospace industry possible. In
    1906 the German metallurgist Alfred Wilm was hardening an aluminium alloy with
    about 4% copper when he noticed something no equilibrium diagram of the day
    predicted: after quenching, the alloy <em>kept strengthening for days at room
    temperature</em>. The explanation had to wait for X-ray work in 1938: the quench
    had trapped a metastable supersaturated solid solution, and tiny copper-rich
    clusters (Guinier–Preston zones) were slowly precipitating — crossing the solvus
    line in slow motion — and obstructing dislocations far more effectively than the
    coarse equilibrium phase ever could. Duralumin went from mystery to Zeppelin
    frames to modern aircraft skins, and age hardening remains metallurgy's most
    valuable piece of <em>non-equilibrium</em> behaviour — invisible on the equilibrium
    diagram except as the solvus that makes it possible.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="rebuild the discovery that ran ahead of the map" widget="order" config={pdWilmOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the map, the rule that pins phase compositions at each
    temperature, and the solubility limits where strengthening begins — with Wilm's alloy as
    the reminder that the most valuable states can lie off the equilibrium map. Next: the
    arithmetic that tells you how much of each phase you actually have.
  </p>

  <h2>The lever rule, with numbers</h2>
  <p>
    In a two-phase region the tie-line is a seesaw and the overall composition is the fulcrum.
    Worked example: an alloy of overall composition 50% B, held in a field where the α phase
    is 20% B and the β phase is 70% B. Mass balance gives the fraction of β:
    f(β) = (C₀ − Cα)/(Cβ − Cα) = (50 − 20)/(70 − 20) = 30/50 = 60%; the fraction of α is
    (70 − 50)/50 = 40%. Check: 0.6 × 70 + 0.4 × 20 = 42 + 8 = 50 — mass conserved. The rule's
    lever name is literal: the phase whose composition is <em>farther</em> from the overall
    composition takes the larger fraction. Now the payoff for steel: a 0.76% C alloy cooling
    through the eutectoid, with ferrite at 0.022% C and cementite at 6.7% C, gives
    f(cementite) ≈ (0.76 − 0.022)/(6.7 − 0.022) ≈ 0.11 — about 11% cementite. Eleven percent
    hard carbide in a soft iron matrix: that proportion, and its fineness of dispersion, is
    most of what the words "steel grade" mean.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the carbide fraction of eutectoid steel" widget="estimate" config={pdCementiteEstimate} />
  <p>
    One more reading, because both exams and plant floors ask for it: the same alloy
    held at 40% B in that same field (α at 20%, β at 70%) gives f(β) = (40 − 20)/50 =
    40% — slide the overall composition toward α's end of the tie-line and the β
    fraction falls. That sensitivity is the lever rule's practical meaning: near the
    ends of a two-phase field, small changes in overall composition swing phase
    fractions quickly, which is why alloy specifications hold narrow composition
    windows.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the tie-line as a seesaw — mass balance giving phase fractions,
    and steel's 11 per cent cementite falling out of one line drawing. Some points on the
    map, though, are special in a way you can taste in every soldered joint.
  </p>

  <h2>Eutectics: the compositions civilisations cast</h2>
  <p>
    Most liquids freeze over a range of temperatures, growing crystals while the remaining
    melt enriches — a mushy, pasty interval that is miserable for casting (dendrites block
    moulds, porosity forms). The <strong>eutectic</strong> composition is the exception: there
    the liquid transforms to two solids <em>simultaneously at one temperature</em>, the lowest
    melting point on the diagram. Tin–lead solder's classic 63/37 blend sits at the eutectic
    (183 °C): it solidifies instantly and sharply, which is why it made reliable joints.
    Cast iron's 4.3% carbon eutectic (1147 °C) melts far below pure iron's 1538 °C — the
    reason iron could be melted and cast for two millennia before steel could be forged at
    scale. The eutectic microstructure is characteristic: alternating fine lamellae of the two
    phases, growing cooperatively because each phase rejects the other's element, and finer
    lamellae (faster cooling) mean stronger material — microstructure engineering begins here.
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "eutectic" alloys are special mixtures with special atoms.
    Any element pair with limited solid solubility shows one; the eutectic is geometry of the
    free-energy curves, not chemistry magic.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="set cast iron's eutectic on the melting scale" widget="place" config={pdEutecticPlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the eutectic — the composition where freezing happens at one
    temperature, which is why solder and cast iron served civilisations for centuries before
    science explained them. Now the diagram that made metallurgy a science, and the narrow
    corridor of it that steel occupies.
  </p>

  <h2>Iron–carbon: the map the Industrial Revolution was drawn on</h2>
  <p>
    Three phases carry the story. <strong>Ferrite</strong> (α-iron) is BCC iron holding
    almost no carbon (0.022% at 727 °C) — soft, ductile, magnetic.
    <strong>Austenite</strong> (γ-iron) is FCC and dissolves up to ~2.1% carbon — it exists
    only at high temperature, and its appetite for carbon is the secret of steelmaking:
    heat steel and the carbon vanishes into solution. <strong>Cementite</strong> (Fe₃C) is a
    brittle iron-carbide compound at 6.7% C — extremely hard. Carbon steels are the 0.02–2%
    carbon corridor of this map. Cool slowly and austenite decomposes at the eutectoid point
    (0.76% C, 727 °C) into <strong>pearlite</strong> — the lamellar ferrite–cementite
    sandwich named for its sheen — whose coarseness (set by cooling rate) tunes strength.
    The diagram's deepest lesson is that <em>heat treatment is travel</em>: every steel
    treatment — annealing, normalising, quenching — is a route drawn on this map, and the next
    lesson follows those routes, including the one that leaves equilibrium entirely
    (martensite exists on no equilibrium diagram: it is what happens when you outrun the map).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="file each property with its iron–carbon phase" widget="sort-buckets" config={pdFeCBuckets} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> more carbon means a better steel, so the
    highest-carbon grade is the strongest choice. Past about 0.8–0.9% C the extra
    carbon mostly makes more brittle cementite, and high-carbon steels trade
    weldability, toughness and ductility for hardness — which is why structural frames
    stay low in carbon, springs and rails sit mid-range, and only blades go high. The
    diagram is a menu of trade-offs, not a scoreboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Batteries: phase diagrams read as voltage</h2>
  <p>
    A lithium-ion battery stores charge by <strong>intercalation</strong> — lithium ions
    slipping into crystal lattices — so every electrode is a host crystal plus a mobile
    component: a small binary system, and its phase behaviour shows up directly in the
    voltage curve. The cell voltage is the difference in lithium <strong>chemical
    potential</strong> between the two electrodes. Now apply the phase rule: when an
    electrode sits in a <strong>two-phase field</strong> — crystals of Li-poor and Li-rich
    composition coexisting — the phase rule (F = 1 with T fixed) pins each phase's
    composition and hence pins the chemical potential. A pinned chemical potential is a
    <strong>flat voltage plateau</strong>. LiFePO₄, the safest commercial cathode, reacts via
    two-phase coexistence between LiFePO₄ and FePO₄ and therefore charges along a famously
    flat ~3.45 V plateau — the lever rule drawn in volts. Layered NMC cathodes, by contrast,
    take lithium as a continuous <strong>solid solution</strong>, so their voltage glides
    smoothly downward as lithium leaves — no plateau, and a fuel-gauge that reads composition
    directly from voltage. Graphite anodes show the subtler <strong>staging</strong> behaviour:
    lithium occupies galleries between graphene sheets in discrete stage compounds (every
    nth layer filled, down to fully stoichiometric LiC₆ at ~0.1 V vs Li), producing a staircase
    of small plateaus. The battery engineer's design space — plateau flatness, voltage level,
    capacity, rate capability — is an electrode phase diagram plus kinetics; degradation
    (crack formation during two-phase reactions, plating when charging outruns diffusion) is
    phase-boundary motion gone wrong.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="which voltage curve is the two-phase cathode?" widget="trace" config={pdPlateauTrace} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> battery voltage is a fuel gauge — it reads the
    amount of charge left. Voltage is a chemical-<em>potential</em> difference, and
    for a two-phase electrode it stays nearly flat across most of the charge
    (LiFePO₄ moves perhaps a hundredth of a volt across its whole plateau), which is
    why such cells need coulomb counting rather than voltmeter-reading to report
    state of charge. The voltmeter is reading the phase diagram, not the tank level.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="walk the fuel-gauge decision" widget="branch" config={pdGaugeBranch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the Gibbs phase rule at constant pressure, and use it to explain why a two-phase field pins the composition of each phase.</li>
    <li>An alloy at 40% B sits in the worked example's field (α at 20% B, β at 70% B). Compute the β fraction, and say what happens to it as the overall composition slides toward α's end of the tie-line.</li>
    <li>Why does a LiFePO₄ cathode charge along a flat voltage plateau, and why does that make a voltmeter a poor fuel gauge?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) F = C − P + 1; for a binary alloy (C = 2) in a two-phase
    field (P = 2), F = 1 — pick the temperature and each phase's composition is fixed by the
    tie-line's ends; only the proportions vary with overall composition. (2) f(β) =
    (40 − 20)/(70 − 20) = 40%; slide the fulcrum toward α's end and β shrinks toward zero —
    near tie-line ends, small composition changes swing phase fractions quickly, which is why
    alloy specifications hold narrow windows. (3) Two solid phases coexist in the electrode,
    so the phase rule pins lithium's chemical potential — and a pinned potential is a flat
    plateau of about 3.45 V. The voltmeter is reading the phase diagram, not the tank level,
    so state of charge needs coulomb counting instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A phase diagram is a thermodynamic map: temperature and composition in, equilibrium phases out.</li>
    <li>The phase rule pins phase compositions in two-phase fields; only the proportions vary with overall composition.</li>
    <li>The lever rule turns a tie-line into phase fractions — a seesaw with the overall composition as fulcrum.</li>
    <li>Eutectics freeze at one temperature and cast cleanly, which is why solder and cast iron served before theory existed.</li>
    <li>Iron–carbon's three phases — ferrite, austenite, cementite — plus the eutectoid are what "steel grade" means.</li>
    <li>Heat treatment is travel on the map; martensite is what happens when you outrun it.</li>
    <li>Battery plateaus are two-phase fields read as voltage — the voltmeter reads the map, not the tank.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Heat treatment — the lesson this one keeps pointing toward — turns the map's routes into
    itineraries: diffusion, TTT diagrams, and the martensite that exists on no equilibrium
    map. Failure, fracture and fatigue extends the strengthening story to its limits, and
    Semiconductors and doping applies the same crystal chemistry to the most profitable
    impurity on Earth.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Where diagrams come from.</strong> Classical diagrams were measured by thermal
    analysis (arrests in cooling curves mark transformations) and microscopy. Modern practice
    is <strong>CALPHAD</strong> (CALculation of PHAse Diagrams): parametrised free-energy
    models for each phase, fitted to experiment, extrapolated to multi-component space no
    human can draw — six-element superalloys and high-entropy alloys are screened
    computationally in weeks, with experiments confirming rather than exploring. The same
    thermodynamic machinery, coupled with kinetics models, powers alloy design from jet-engine
    <strong>superalloys</strong> (whose γ′ strengthening phase is engineered to dissolve just
    above operating temperature) to <strong>stainless steel</strong> (chromium above ~12%
    forms the self-healing oxide film; nickel stabilises tough austenite at room temperature —
    the 18/8 of cutlery shifts the phase field, not just the corrosion).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Equilibrium is the map, not the destination.</strong> Phase diagrams describe
    equilibrium — where the system would go given infinite time. Engineering frequently
    profits by <em>refusing</em> to arrive: quenched martensite, metastable precipitates,
    amorphous glasses and undercooled liquids are all off-map states. The quantitative
    question — "how fast must I cool to freeze in this metastable state?" — is the bridge
    between thermodynamics (this lesson) and kinetics (the next lesson on heat treatment),
    and it is governed by diffusion distances and nucleation barriers. The TTT diagrams of
    the next lesson are phase diagrams with a third axis smuggled in: time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Two-phase battery reactions deform their host crystals
    as phase boundaries sweep through — the coherency strains and crack patterns that limit
    cycle life are an active computational and experimental frontier (why does LiFePO₄
    sometimes react via a metastable solid solution at high current, "suppressing" the
    plateau?). High-entropy alloys with four or more principal elements strain classical
    intuition, and machine-learned interatomic potentials are now fast enough to compute
    phase diagrams from first principles — reopening the question of what a phase diagram is
    for when you can simulate the whole processing route.
  </p>

